# The fusions that call the double-counting question malformed, with the parts they actually
# have. One row per composed layer filing its elimination search as `notApplicable`, carrying
# how many parts it names.
#
# When `notApplicable` is right
#   Between a set of one, nothing can be counted twice, so `notApplicable` is exactly right on a
#   single-part fusion and the question genuinely does not arise. Between two or more it does
#   arise: `notApplicable` there is a claim that two supplies cannot possibly overlap, which is a
#   judgement about the world and belongs in `observed` where somebody signs it.
#
# The part count is the whole population test
#   A `notApplicable` search on a fusion with no parts filed at all is not examined by the rule and
#   must not be counted as passing it, which is why a fusion whose count is zero is left out rather
#   than carried with a zero the rule would read.
-- eliminations/searched.sqlc answering "notApplicable", beside folds/fusion_parts.sqlc.
SELECT es.composition, es.composed_layer, f.parts
FROM (
    :compose(eliminations/searched.sqlc)
) es
JOIN (
    :compose(folds/fusion_parts.sqlc)
) f
  ON f.composition = es.composition AND f.composed_layer = es.composed_layer
WHERE es.answer = 'notApplicable'
  AND f.parts > 0
